# Root cause: ingest-partition-skew

BEYOND-CONTEXT / MONOREPO (adversarial, chunk 2). 267 commits over 72h; log_rps 0.45 produces well over 8MB of telemetry across logs/metrics/traces/patterns — the data must be sliced, not read whole. Culprit is 'refactor(ingest): stable partitioning for tenant affinity' (services/event-producer/partition.go), authored 2026-07-25T17:20Z and deployed to event-producer at 17:50Z, ~20h before the alert fires at 2026-07-26T13:50Z. The diff swaps the partition-key field: `hash(msg.DeviceID)` becomes `hash(msg.TenantID)` — the comment only mentions 'per-tenant windowed aggregation', never skew or overload, so the fault must be read from the code: routing by tenant instead of device means every event from one whale tenant now hashes to the SAME partition instead of being spread across devices. The mechanism is only visible as a DERIVED signature, never named directly: kafka-metric-ingestor exposes eight per-partition lag gauges, consumer_lag_partition_0 through _7. Partitions 0-6 stay flat at their own baselines (210-380, jitter 0.15) for the entire window — direct proof the skew is confined to one partition, not a global consumer problem. consumer_lag_partition_7 alone ramps from a baseline of 300 starting 2026-07-25T18:50Z (1h after the culprit's own deploy) up to a ~900,000 plateau by 2026-07-26T09:50Z — a 15h ramp that is flatly inconsistent with any of the consumer-side decoys below, all of which deploy well after that ramp is already established. messages_consumed_per_sec (kafka-metric-ingestor) independently corroborates: it starts declining from its 850 baseline toward ~400 at 2026-07-26T05:00Z, hours before the alerting service shows any sign of trouble — this is why first_failing_service is kafka-metric-ingestor even though the causal commit shipped to event-producer. chart_data_staleness_s (dashboard-svc), by contrast, stays near its 45s baseline for nearly the whole window: staleness only becomes threshold-crossing once the backlog on partition 7 is large enough to starve the downstream refresh job, so it is flat until onset (2026-07-26T11:50Z, chosen where the alerting metric's own ramp visibly accelerates) and then ramps to a ~1400s plateau by 13:55Z, crossing the 900s threshold a few minutes before the alert fires — an ~18h delayed onset relative to the actual cause. Two WARN log_faults on kafka-metric-ingestor ('rebalance triggered: max poll interval exceeded') establish the timeline in the data itself: a low precursor rate (0.2/min, count 196) runs from 2026-07-25T19:30Z — 40 minutes after the lag ramp begins and many hours before either mechanism-trap deploys — then jumps to 4/min (count 480) at onset. dashboard-svc's own victim pattern ('chart render served stale data: upstream ingest lag exceeded refresh window', 6/min from 2026-07-26T11:55Z, count 750) outnumbers kafka-metric-ingestor's onset-linked WARN pattern (480), satisfying victim-first pattern loudness. Three hand-authored trace exemplars make the mechanism directly visible: a healthy pre-deploy exemplar (2026-07-24T10:00Z) shows PublishBatch(45ms,OK) -> ConsumeBatch(55ms,OK) -> RefreshChartData(28ms,OK); two independent post-onset exemplars (2026-07-26T13:40Z and 13:47Z) show ConsumeBatch spans blowing up to ~41-45 seconds, erroring 'poll interval exceeded while draining partition 7 backlog; rebalance triggered', which propagates directly into RefreshChartData spans erroring 'chart data stale: upstream ingest lag exceeded refresh window' — the derived partition-7 signature corroborated across two independent traces, with no numeric fingerprint from any diff quoted in the text. Five mechanism-traps deploy pre-onset (except innocent-near-alert, which is post-onset bait) and are each exonerable purely by code semantics, not timing: (1) kafka-bump-decoy ('chore(consumer): bump kafka client 3.6->3.7', deployed 2026-07-26T05:50Z, 8h pre-alert) touches go.mod plus one renamed constant, `kafka.RangeAssignor` -> `kafka.RangeAssignorName` — the client's v3.7 release